Effects of capital movement (MacDougall analysis)
Capital flowing from a capital-rich to a capital-scarce country raises world output. The host gains from higher output and wages; the home country gains from higher returns; world efficiency rises. The home labour force loses as capital is exported, but the home nation as a whole gains.
Benefits of FDI to the host country
• Fills the savings and foreign exchange gap.
• Transfers technology, management and market access.
• Creates employment and linkages with local firms.
• Raises exports and tax revenue.
Costs to the host country
• Profit repatriation worsens the current account.
• Crowding out of domestic firms; market power of multinationals.
• Transfer pricing and tax avoidance.
• Enclave development, environmental harm and loss of sovereignty.
Benefits to the home country
Higher profits, access to raw materials and markets, expansion of exports of inputs, and acquisition of knowledge.
Costs to the home country
Job loss (export of jobs), lower domestic investment, balance of payments pressure from capital outflow, and loss of technology advantage.
Conclusion
FDI is on balance beneficial and more stable than FPI, but gains depend on host policies: absorptive capacity, regulation and linkages decide whether the benefits are realised.